 ## Code Is the Documentation
 
-- Code is the documentation, not comments. Make the code self-explanatory
-  through clear names, small functions, and obvious structure.
-- Adding a comment is the absolute bare exception, never the norm. Do not
-  narrate what the code does, add section-header comments, or restate the
-  obvious — delete such comments when you see them.
-- The only justifiable comment is genuinely non-obvious *why* (rationale, a
-  gotcha, an external constraint) that the code itself cannot express — and even
-  then, first try to make the code clearer instead.
+Make code self-explanatory through clear names, small functions, and obvious structure, so it needs no narrating comments. Write a comment only for a non-obvious *why* — rationale, a gotcha, an external constraint — that clearer code cannot express. Remove comments that restate the code or act as section headers when you touch that code.
 
